  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>What EMDR does</h2>
      <p>Some experiences get stored the way they arrived: raw, out of sequence, still carrying
      the body state that came with them. A sound, a smell or a tone of voice reaches that
      storage directly, and you are back in it before you have decided anything. EMDR gives the
      brain the conditions to file the material properly, so the memory stays and the hijack
      stops.</p>
      <p>The dual attention task, following eye movements or taps while holding the material, is
      the part everyone has heard of. It is not hypnosis and it is not a trance. You stay awake,
      aware, and able to stop at any point.</p>
      <h3>Stabilization comes first</h3>
      <p>Reem's emphasis is the preparation phases: acute stress, ongoing stress, feeling
      overwhelmed, loss. Only with that footing does processing become useful rather than
      destabilizing. This is also the most common reason people arrive here saying EMDR did not
      work for them, and it is usually a preparation problem rather than a protocol failure.</p>
      <h3>It tends to fit when</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>Specific memories intrude, or certain triggers reliably hijack you.</li>
        <li>You are in an ongoing stressful situation and need footing before insight.</li>
        <li>Talking about it has stopped helping.</li>
        <li>You want the charge out of something, not a better account of it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h3>On the controversy</h3>
      <p>Worth stating plainly, since it is the thing people read about. The outcomes are not in
      dispute: EMDR is recommended for PTSD by major bodies and trials consistently show it
      helps. The mechanism is. Dismantling studies that strip out the eye movements often find
      similar results, which suggests the bilateral stimulation may matter less than the
      structured exposure and reprocessing around it. It works, and the field does not fully
      agree on why.</p>
      <h3>The protocols</h3>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>ASSYST</dt><dd>A brief protocol for recent and ongoing stress</dd></div>
        <div><dt>PRECI</dt><dd>Protocol for recent critical incidents</dd></div>
        <div><dt>IGTP-OTS</dt><dd>A group protocol, used for ongoing traumatic stress</dd></div>
        <div><dt>IFS-informed</dt><dd><a href="/approach/ifs-informed-emdr/">For developmental trauma, where protectors object</a></dd></div>
      </dl>
