<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about EMDR</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Can I do EMDR therapy online in Texas?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes, and it is the default here. Bilateral stimulation adapts to video using
      self-administered tapping or on-screen movement, and the preparation work is unaffected.
      Both you and your therapist must be physically located in Texas during the session.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What are the eight phases of EMDR?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>History taking, preparation, assessment, desensitization, installation, body scan,
      closure and reevaluation. The first two are where this practice spends most of its effort,
      because insufficient preparation is the usual reason the later phases fail.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does EMDR make you relive the trauma?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. You hold the material in mind while attending to something else, which is the
      opposite of immersion, and the dual attention is what keeps one foot in the present. If you
      are flooding, the pacing is wrong and that is information rather than failure.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>What is an EMDRIA Approved Consultant?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>A clinician approved by the EMDR International Association to provide the consultation
      hours other therapists need for EMDR certification. It is a credential about teaching EMDR,
      not only practicing it, and it requires certification plus substantial supervised
      consultation experience.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is EMDR covered by insurance?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Insurers reimburse by session code rather than by method, so an EMDR session bills as
      individual psychotherapy. Blue Cross Blue Shield is accepted here; sessions under any other
      plan are self-pay at $240 for 50 minutes.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Who should not do EMDR?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Nobody is ruled out by diagnosis, but timing matters. Active crisis, an unstable living
      situation, or having no reliable way to settle yourself afterward are all reasons to build
      footing first rather than start processing. That judgment is part of what the consult and
      the preparation phases are for.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
